WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.440
Foretelling text messages, ‘The&nbsp;
Silence of The Lambs’ inspiration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:10.020
bizarre tirades and piano drops. “Two And A Half&nbsp;
Men”’s ending isn’t as simple as you’d think.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:15.660
In "Of Course He's Dead," Walden receives&nbsp;
a text from Charlie, which is a lengthy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:20.700
rant against him. This rant contains plenty&nbsp;
of strange comments, including the phrases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:26.340
"bayonets of truth," "an army of assassins,"&nbsp;
and allusions to Charlie's "tiger blood."

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:31.020
Many of these phrases are taken almost verbatim&nbsp;
from comments Charlie Sheen made following his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:35.880
departure from "Two and a Half Men." The "bayonets&nbsp;
of truth" line is likely meant as a parody of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:42.480
Sheen's live tour, "Fastball; My Violent Torpedo&nbsp;
of Truth/Defeat is Not an Option," announced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:47.880
shortly after his contract's termination with CBS.&nbsp;
The "army of assassins" he threatens Walden with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:52.620
is a reference to Sheen's previous comments on a&nbsp;
media show where the actor claimed the following:

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:56.340
"There's a new sheriff in town.&nbsp;
And he has an army of assassins."

00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:01.260
The line about Charlie's "tiger's blood"&nbsp;
is an obvious riff on Sheen's now infamous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:06.060
interview with ABC News. After discussing his&nbsp;
past use of alcohol and drugs, Sheen said:

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:07.860
"I just have a different constitution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:11.880
I have a different brain, I have a&nbsp;
different heart. I got tiger blood, man."

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:15.720
Charlie's text to Walden, calling him&nbsp;
a "troll," may also be a reference to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:20.280
Sheen's past comments about Jon Cryer after&nbsp;
his dismissal, in which he referred to Cryer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:24.420
as a troll among other names for not&nbsp;
defending him from CBS and Chuck Lorre.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:29.400
In part two of the finale, Alan and Walden&nbsp;
go to the police to ask for their assistance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:34.620
While meeting with police Lieutenant Wagner, the&nbsp;
two provide a summary of the entire series in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:39.240
meta dialogue. After Walden underscores&nbsp;
just how dangerous Charlie's rage is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:44.940
Wagner asks the duo if Charlie ever tried&nbsp;
anger management. This leads Alan to quip:

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:46.320
"Yeah, but it didn't work."

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:50.820
This joke is a dig at Charlie Sheen's&nbsp;
FX series, "Anger Management," which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:54.720
he worked on after "Two and a Half Men."&nbsp;
The premise of the series was meant as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:59.340
somewhat self-aware meditation on Sheen's&nbsp;
own rage issues, with the actor playing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:03.600
former baseball player whose career comes to&nbsp;
an end because of his uncontrollable anger.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:08.640
The sitcom was Sheen's first acting role after&nbsp;
"Two and a Half Men," and was meant as his return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:13.560
to TV after his fallout with Chuck Lorre. While&nbsp;
"Anger Management" ran for a healthy two seasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:18.120
it earned mixed to negative reviews&nbsp;
from critics. Some unfavorably compared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:22.500
it to Sheen's previous sitcom role.&nbsp;
Shortly after its 100th episode aired,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:27.120
the show was canceled by FX, leading to Alan's&nbsp;
joke in "Two and a Half Men"'s final episode.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:32.880
At the start of "Of Course He's Dead," Rose&nbsp;
is shown preparing a meal and descending into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:36.720
her basement, where a large pit is seen&nbsp;
in the middle of the floor. Placing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.560
meal into a basket and lowering it into&nbsp;
the hole, she tells the unseen character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:45.240
whom she refers to as "it" to eat the meal&nbsp;
she prepared and to change his clothes.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:48.240
Of course, it is revealed that it's Charlie in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.540
pit. When Charlie appears reluctant&nbsp;
to do either of Rose's requests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:55.560
she threatens to spray him with a large&nbsp;
fire hose until he complies. This entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:00.000
scene is meant as a direct parody of the Academy&nbsp;
Award-winning film, "The Silence of the Lambs."

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.560
In that film, the serial killer antagonist&nbsp;
known as Buffalo Bill keeps his victims in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:08.760
a large pit located in his basement, very&nbsp;
much like Rose's own pit in "Two and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:12.360
Half Men." Buffalo Bill also takes&nbsp;
to addressing his victims with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:17.040
dehumanizing "it" and threatens to spray&nbsp;
them with a hose should they disobey him.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:21.240
"It rubs the lotion on its skin&nbsp;
or it gets the hose again."

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:25.920
The influence "The Silence of the Lambs"&nbsp;
has on the finale doesn't stop there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:30.900
either. During the finale's animated flashback&nbsp;
sequence, Rose is seen transporting Charlie from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:36.540
Paris on a metal gurney, with Charlie restrained&nbsp;
via a straight jacket and muzzle. This is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:41.520
exact same manner that Anthony Hopkins' iconic&nbsp;
serial killer, Hannibal Lecter, is transported.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:46.560
Halfway through the finale, a montage&nbsp;
shows several women from Charlie's past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:51.720
receiving personal apology letters and&nbsp;
large checks in the mail. Three of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:55.560
four women played larger roles earlier&nbsp;
in "Two and a Half Men," two of whom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.560 --> 00:04:00.060
Charlie even came dramatically close to&nbsp;
marrying in the series' initial seasons.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:04.260
During this montage, viewers see&nbsp;
Charlie's former girlfriends, Mia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:09.840
Chelsea, and Dolores Pasternak. Mia was a ballet&nbsp;
teacher who Charlie fell in love with in Season 3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:14.280
of "Two and a Half Men" and became a regular&nbsp;
romantic interest for Charlie from Seasons 5&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:18.180
through 7. She was also his fiancée for a&nbsp;
short time in Season 3, although Charlie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:22.260
decided to break off the wedding after Mia&nbsp;
tried kicking Alan and Jake out of the house.

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:27.300
Chelsea was Charlie's girlfriend throughout&nbsp;
Season 6, eventually becoming his fiancée in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:31.560
Season 7. Their engagement was impacted&nbsp;
by a number of personal issues and side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:35.760
romances on both of their parts, resulting in&nbsp;
Chelsea breaking up with Charlie in the end.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:40.800
Introduced in Season 2, Dolores Pasternak was&nbsp;
one of Jake's teachers, who Charlie similarly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:45.540
dated for a short time. The character went&nbsp;
on to appear again in Season 6 and Season 7,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:49.020
although she was played by a different&nbsp;
actor. All three women were also featured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:53.040
at Charlie's funeral service in&nbsp;
the opening moments of Season 9.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:58.500
Alongside Charlie's past romantic interests,&nbsp;
several old flames of both Alan and Walden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:03.180
are also featured in "Of Course He's Dead."&nbsp;
As they anxiously await Charlie's vengeance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:07.140
each of them dials up some of their&nbsp;
long-running significant others. For Alan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:10.560
this includes ex-girlfriend&nbsp;
Lyndsey, and his two ex-wives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:15.900
Judith and Kandi. For Walden, this includes&nbsp;
ex-wife Bridget and ex-girlfriend Zoey.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:20.820
All of these women played big roles during&nbsp;
"Two and a Half Men." Before the events of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:24.540
the series, Judith and Alan maintained an&nbsp;
unhappy marriage with each other. Their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:28.560
subsequent divorce caused Alan to move into&nbsp;
Charlie's beach house in the pilot episode.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:33.180
Kandi was one of Charlie's many one-off&nbsp;
girlfriends in Season 3 who later dated Alan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:36.540
becoming his second wife soon&nbsp;
after. They divorced in Season 4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:42.000
with Kandi enjoying a successful career as a&nbsp;
television star. Lyndsey was Alan's on-again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:45.900
off-again girlfriend during the final few&nbsp;
seasons of the series, reluctantly becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:50.220
engaged to Alan after seeing the massive&nbsp;
ring Walden purchased on Alan's behalf.

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:54.720
Bridget was Walden's wife prior to his debut&nbsp;
on the show, divorcing him because of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:58.920
supposed immaturity. However, she once again&nbsp;
developed feelings for him after the divorce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:03.900
was finalized. After the end of his marriage to&nbsp;
Bridget, Walden then becomes attached to Zoey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:08.460
but the two break up when Zoey learns of&nbsp;
Walden's romance with Rose in Season 10.

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:12.960
Along with the several sizable checks&nbsp;
he sends to his ex-girlfriends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:18.600
Charlie also mails a check to Jake for&nbsp;
$250,000. Likely, Charlie giving Jake such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:22.740
a large amount of money is meant to reflect&nbsp;
his general warm feelings toward his nephew.

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:26.400
Much of the earlier seasons of "Two and&nbsp;
a Half Men" find Charlie and Jake bonding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:30.000
in a variety of past-times together.&nbsp;
Taking his young nephew under his wing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.160
Charlie also encouraged Jake to learn from his&nbsp;
own mistakes in life and avoid repeating them.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:39.060
As Jake grew older and became increasingly&nbsp;
distant from his father and uncle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:44.340
Charlie was seen to treat him less warmly than&nbsp;
he had in previous seasons. Still, he admitted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:49.800
to harboring a fond place for Jake a few times as&nbsp;
the show went on, usually when he was inebriated.

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:54.300
Charlie sending Jake money illustrates that&nbsp;
he still holds some love for his nephew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:56.880
leaving their relationship&nbsp;
on a high note in the finale.

00:06:57.420 --> 00:06:58.620
"Okay, you got me."

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:04.740
After receiving Charlie's check for $250,000,&nbsp;
Jake tells Alan and Walden that he stopped at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:10.320
Las Vegas before visiting them, winning a total&nbsp;
of $2.5 million from playing craps. In many ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:14.520
this might appear like a throwaway joke meant&nbsp;
to provide Jake a fitting epilogue for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:18.300
character, but it also ties in greatly to his&nbsp;
relationship with his uncle Charlie as well.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:22.560
In the early seasons of "Two and a Half Men,"&nbsp;
Charlie was shown teaching Jake various adult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:27.660
activities, such as poker. From Jake watching his&nbsp;
uncle play Texas Hold 'em with his friends to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:31.620
two of them betting on sports games together,&nbsp;
Charlie instructed Jake on all the subtleties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:36.180
of gambling from an incredibly young age. As&nbsp;
an adult, it makes sense that Jake learned to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:41.460
become an effective enough gambler to increase&nbsp;
his winnings from $250,000 to $2.5 million.

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:47.280
One of the more unique aspects of "Of Course&nbsp;
He's Dead" is the meta-aware comedic style&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:51.540
of the finale. Over the course of the episode,&nbsp;
numerous jokes are made about the show's content,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:56.700
the ludicrousness of its central storyline and&nbsp;
characters, and the real-life drama surrounding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.700 --> 00:08:01.140
former star Charlie Sheen's behavior in his&nbsp;
personal life. There are even a handful of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.140 --> 00:08:05.460
moments where the actors address the audience&nbsp;
members, regularly breaking the fourth wall.

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:09.120
The creator of "Two and a Half Men,"&nbsp;
Chuck Lorre, has discussed the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:13.200
self-referential humor that the finale&nbsp;
employed with Entertainment Weekly. There,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:16.860
Lorre said that the comedic style of&nbsp;
"Of Course He's Dead" was meant mostly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:20.460
as a way for the show to conclude in&nbsp;
a humorous way. The showrunner said:

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:24.240
"It was our last swing at that bat.&nbsp;
Why not throw the rules out and do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:28.620
everything we can imagine that might be funny&nbsp;
and acknowledging the meta elements of the show?"

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:33.300
Lorre also said that he felt the show&nbsp;
didn't warrant a more emotional undertone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:36.360
as there was no central storyline&nbsp;
imparted from the beginning of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:39.120
show that could be effectively&nbsp;
wrapped up with its conclusion.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:42.120
"Yeah, right. And I'm the governor of California."

00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:46.500
The final scene of "Two and a Half Men"&nbsp;
sees Charlie meeting his premature end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:50.400
after a piano crushes him. While Charlie's&nbsp;
character here is played by a body double&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:54.300
and his face never revealed to the camera,&nbsp;
it was initially planned for Charlie Sheen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:57.240
to return to "Two and a Half Men" as&nbsp;
a special guest star for the finale.

00:08:57.240 --> 00:09:02.520
In an interview with EW, Chuck Lorre said&nbsp;
that he and CBS had tried reaching out to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:06.720
Sheen to negotiate his return for "Of Course&nbsp;
He's Dead." However, as Chuck Lorre said on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:11.700
his trademark vanity card for the episode, Sheen&nbsp;
wanted the show to end on a more cathartic note.

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:15.600
He wanted to leave open the possibility of&nbsp;
a spin-off series starring himself and Jon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:20.460
Cryer titled "The Harpers." With Lorre wanting to&nbsp;
maintain a more lighthearted tone for the finale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:25.020
he refused, and the negotiations between&nbsp;
himself and Sheen soon fell apart.

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:29.160
Per Lorre's vanity card for the episode, the&nbsp;
initial plan was to have Charlie turn to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:32.640
camera and deliver a monologue about&nbsp;
the dangers of drug use. Over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:36.120
Charlie's speech would devolve into&nbsp;
a bizarre tirade about how he was "a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:39.600
ninja warrior from Mars" and was&nbsp;
"invincible" until his speech was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:43.980
cut short when a piano crashes on top of&nbsp;
him, as it does in the finished finale.

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:50.100
Chuck Lorre's physical presence and his subsequent&nbsp;
death in the finale can be eyebrow-raising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:54.120
for some viewers. However confusing and&nbsp;
meta Lorre's appearance in the show is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:57.300
though, Lorre has since justified&nbsp;
his cameo at the end of the episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.300 --> 00:10:01.140
as well as elaborating on the&nbsp;
reasoning behind his onscreen demise.

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:05.160
Chatting to Entertainment Weekly, Lorre&nbsp;
said that the piano falling on both of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:09.840
him and Charlie is meant as an illustration of&nbsp;
their rocky relationship and the effect it had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:14.040
on the series. In Lorre's eyes, his feud&nbsp;
with Sheen ultimately led to the show's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:17.880
steady decline as "Two and a Half Men" went&nbsp;
on without Sheen there to headline the show.

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:20.760
The two pianos falling on&nbsp;
both of them were therefore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:23.340
intended to be symbolic, with Lorre saying:

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:27.240
"It felt like the funniest and most&nbsp;
succinct way to end the damn thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:31.860
And dropping the second piano also felt like&nbsp;
an appropriate response -- perhaps nobody wins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:34.020
but hopefully, we laughed along the way."

